<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Granada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was the Spanish name for the lands of present-day Colombia and Panama, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> of Granada founded in 1538. In 1717 it became the seat of the Viceroyalty of New Granada, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and including Venezuela and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, re-established permanently in 1739. During the independence wars its patriots fought the royalists, and in 1819 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-angostura">Congress of Angostura</a> united it with Venezuela as Gran Colombia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 17 December 1819 the Congress of Angostura passed a Fundamental Law proclaiming the Republic of Colombia, a union of Venezuela, <b>New Granada</b> and the still unconquered Presidency of Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Muisca heartland became the core of the New Kingdom of Granada: Santa Fe de Bogotá, founded in 1538 on the savanna of the zipas, became the seat of a royal audiencia created in 1549, and later the capital of the Viceroyalty of <b>New Granada</b> and of Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Ecuador grew out of the Royal Audiencia of Quito, the Spanish colonial jurisdiction covering the northern Andes between <b>New Granada</b> and Peru.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pétion's republic gave Simón Bolívar the arms and money that let him liberate <b>New Granada</b>, and Haiti ruled the whole island from 1822 to 1844, ending slavery in Santo Domingo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The isthmus of Panama, ruled by Spain until 1821, joined Gran Colombia after independence and remained part of its successors, <b>New Granada</b> and later Colombia, despite attempts at secession in 1830, 1831 and 1840–1841.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The viceroyalties of New Spain (1535) and Peru (1542) were the primary administrative units, later joined by <b>New Granada</b> (1717, re-established 1739) and Río de la Plata (1776), each headed by a viceroy of high rank who exercised near-royal authority subject to the oversight of the audiencias (colonial…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
